        <strong>+£{fmt(model.costsVsSavings.scenarios[0].tenYearNet_m)}m</strong> to
        <strong>+£{fmt(model.costsVsSavings.scenarios[2].tenYearNet_m)}m</strong>, a twenty-fold difference,
        and payback moves from {model.costsVsSavings.scenarios[0].payback} to {model.costsVsSavings.scenarios[2].payback}.
        Which scenario Lancashire gets is decided by execution: whether pay harmonisation is done before vesting
        (North Yorkshire) or left to drift (Somerset), and whether the county split is managed better than Cumbria's
        and Northamptonshire's. For comparison, Reform UK's earlier analysis put the enhanced status quo at ~£552m
        over ten years with no transition risk at all.
      </p>
      <p class="doge-note" style="font-size:0.78rem;">
        Method: deterministic scenario model, all assumptions in the open data feed. The £81.9m run-rate is the
        bidding councils' own claim, not verified; the £85.5m equal-pay figure is Reform UK's published modelled
        exposure; realisation rates and cost envelope come from the precedents evidence base. These are scenarios,
        not forecasts, and none includes balance-sheet disaggregation costs, which remain unquantifiable.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Gaps -->
    <section class="doge-section">
      <h2 class="doge-h2">What isn't confirmed yet</h2>
      <p class="doge-note">
        In keeping with the rule that every published figure must be traceable, these items are not yet verified
        against a published source and are deliberately left open rather than estimated:
      </p>
      <div class="doge-panel">
        <ul class="lgr-gaps">
          <li><strong>Official transition costing</strong> for the four-unitary configuration has not been published. The £50m to £100m envelope above is a precedent read-across, not a government figure.</li>
          <li><strong>How council tax will actually be harmonised</strong>: the legal framework is settled, the choice is not. The equalisation power lets a new unitary charge different amounts in predecessor areas for up to seven years, with a single uniform Band D mandatory by year eight (SI 2008/3022 Part 4, extended by SI 2018/1296). Because a new authority has no prior year of its own, the Secretary of State lays an Alternative Notional Amount under s.52ZE of the Local Government Finance Act 1992 as the referendum comparator, and a restructuring authority may test its increase either against each predecessor area or against the average across them. Of the four 2023 unitaries, Somerset and Cumberland harmonised on day one and North Yorkshire phased over two years. What has not been published is which path Lancashire takes: that is a decision for the shadow councils and government.</li>
          <li><strong>The second-home premium clock is already running</strong>: a first determination under s.11C(3) must be made at least one year before the financial year it applies to, and SI 2026/406 (in force 8 May 2026) treats a successor council's determination as a first determination for any area whose predecessor had not made one. For the premium to apply from vesting on 1 April 2028 in Ribble Valley, Fylde, Wyre, Hyndburn, Rossendale and Burnley, a determination must be made by 31 March 2027, before the shadow authorities are elected. No council has published one.</li>
          <li><strong>Balance-sheet disaggregation</strong>: how LCC's assets (including the £519m bond portfolio), debt, reserves and the £17.3m 2028/29 gap will be split across four successors is not yet determined; Northamptonshire's £953m dispute shows how contested this can be.</li>
          <li><strong>A few Band D figures</strong> (Lancaster, Preston, Ribble Valley, Fylde, Chorley) are sourced from budget papers but await confirmation against the formal council-tax resolution, marked medium-confidence in the data feed.</li>
          <li><strong>The Combined Authority split</strong>: which functions (and what money) end up at CCA rather than unitary level is still being settled; the model's combined budgets will need a CCA carve-out once a published disaggregation exists.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Sources -->
    <section class="doge-section">
      <h2 class="doge-h2">Sources</h2>
      <p class="doge-note" style="font-size:0.8rem;">
        Decision and council groupings: Lancashire County Council / MHCLG news release, 16 July 2026, and
        lancashirelgr.co.uk. Lancashire County Council finances: the council's own 2026/27 budget report (Full
        Council, 26 February 2026) and GOV.UK / MHCLG Revenue Outturn returns, compiled and verified in the AI DOGE
        Lancashire data pack (aidoge.co.uk). Reserves, resilience, contracts and property figures are drawn from
        published transparency data. Population per unitary is summed from ONS Census 2021 per-authority totals.
        Spend by directorate and largest suppliers are from the AI DOGE Lancashire pack. Each constituent council's
        net budget and Band D figure is sourced from its own 2026/27 budget papers or council-tax resolution.
        Precedent figures come from MHCLG decision documents, council business cases, KPMG/CIPFA closure and
        assurance reviews, Hansard and audited accounts. Lower-tier political control is a May 2026 snapshot from
        the elections data platform (indicative at status level). Verify against the primary source before any
        formal use. The structural model is the working paper: Pickup, T. (2026) <em>The Financial Case for Local Government Reorganisation in Lancashire: A Transparent Component Model of Five Structural Proposals, with a Postscript on the 2026 Decisions</em>.
        Working paper, version 3.1. Zenodo. Available at: <a href={PAPER.$meta.doiUrl}>{PAPER.$meta.doiUrl}</a>. <strong>The full model</strong>, every input, its source and its confidence rating, <strong>is published as
        open data at <a href="/data/lgr-model.json">/data/lgr-model.json</a>.</strong>
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>

<script>
  function initModelExplorer() {
    const mx = document.querySelector('.lgr-mx');
    if (!mx) return;
    const tabs = Array.from(mx.querySelectorAll('.lgr-mx-tab'));
    const panels = Array.from(mx.querySelectorAll('.lgr-mx-panel'));
    tabs.forEach((tab) => {
      tab.addEventListener('click', () => {
        const i = tab.getAttribute('data-i');
        tabs.forEach((t) => t.classList.toggle('active', t === tab));
        panels.forEach((p) => p.classList.toggle('active', p.getAttribute('data-i') === i));
      });
    });
  }
  initModelExplorer();
  document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', initModelExplorer);
</script>

